  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nInto the long term our temperatures will drop several degrees\nbelow average under a colder airmass with some increasing\nchances for precipitation, primarily for our eastern zones this\ntime, with temperatures then warming to near average by the\nmiddle of the work week under brief clearing. The upper level\npattern will see the northern trough quickly accelerating\neastwards, leaving us under southwesterly flow aloft from a\nbrief finger of weak transient ridging that will quickly be \nerased by low pressure slowly progressing across the Four \nCorners region. This will then become our main weather feature \nthrough Monday before finally sliding eastwards into the Central\nPlains, with another transient ridge building on Tuesday that \nwill briefly hold the region before another Pacific low moves \ninto the Pacific Northwest and then starts to knock on our \ndoorstep by Wednesday into Thursday. Under this pattern, we \nshould see surface flow begin to weaken by later Saturday and \ncontinue into the early part of next week as the strongest part \nof the jet remains just south and west of the forecast area. \nWith this flow, the best moisture will remain just outside of \nour forecast area to the east as well. That being said, PWAT \nvalues will still be on the higher side even for our area by \naround 0.15-0.25 inch, and scattered shower activity is expected\nfrom Saturday afternoon and continuing through Monday evening \nbefore exiting the region. The highest QPF amounts should align \nwith the best moisture for the southeastern portions of the \nNebraska Panhandle, but some meager moisture should occur \nacross most of the forecast area east of the Laramie Range. \nMorning lows both Sunday and Monday could also allow for this to\nmix with snow, but don't expect noteworthy winter accumulations\nat this time. As conditions finally clear later Monday into \nTuesday and the transient ridge moves in, 700mb temperatures \nwill warm back into the 5-8 C range, allowing surface \ntemperatures to rise back to near average in the 50-60 degree \nrange. Tightening pressure gradients from the approaching \nPacific system will start to increase westerly to southwesterly \nwinds again for our western zones on Tuesday and Wednesday, but \notherwise expect conditions to be a bit more uneventful as \nprecipitation chances dwindle outside of the high terrain for \nthe middle of the week.",
